<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1656">1656</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach was an Austrian architect, the leading master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he worked for the imperial court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, designing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlskirche">Karlskirche</a>, the Court Library in the Hofburg and the first plans for Schönbrunn Palace, as well as churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>. His Outline of Historical Architecture of 1721 was one of the first comparative histories of world architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-bernhard-fischer-von-erlach.jpg" alt="Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ádám Mányoki · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Bernhard_Fischer_von_Erlach_by_%C3%81d%C3%A1m_M%C3%A1nyoki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and baptised on 20 July 1656. He trained as a sculptor in the workshop of his father, Johann Baptist Fischer, who worked on the decoration of the Landhaus and Eggenberg Palace in Graz. The Princes of Eggenberg, his father's patrons, arranged for the son to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1671, aged sixteen, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and joined the workshop of Johann Paul Schor and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Gian Lorenzo Bernini</a>, and by 1685 he had followed Schor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he served the Spanish viceroy. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1687 he secured the position of court architect, which he kept under three emperors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Commissions were plentiful, as the nobility sought to repair damage done to their country residences in the Ottoman campaign of 1683. In 1690 he built two temporary triumphal arches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> for Joseph I's coronation, and he later taught Joseph architecture; in 1696 he was raised to the nobility as von Erlach. His emblematic design of the 1690s was the Winter Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a>, begun in 1695. For the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> he built the Holy Trinity Church (1694 to 1702), the Kollegienkirche (1696 to 1707) and Schloss Klessheim (1700 to 1709), whose domes and towers changed the outline of Salzburg.</p><p>After Joseph I died in 1711 Fischer was rarely given new commissions, since the designs of his rival Johann Lucas von Hildebrandt were more popular with Charles VI, and he took on administrative tasks. The Clam-Gallas Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> was begun in 1713, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlskirche">Karlskirche</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1715; it was completed by his son Joseph Emanuel. Fischer died on 5 April 1723.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In the Karlskirche Fischer sought to harmonise the main elements of the most significant churches in the history of Western architecture: the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, the Pantheon, Saint Peter's Basilica, the Dome des Invalides and Saint Paul's Cathedral. His journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1704 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1707 fed his study of Mansart's country residences and Palladian villas. A visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> one of its first professional views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s Palace. In 1721 he published a groundbreaking book of reconstructions of the buildings of antiquity, one of the first and most popular comparative studies of world architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Habsburg court made Vienna a cultural capital: the Baroque architecture of <b>Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach</b> and Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt, the Karlskirche, the Belvedere and the abbey of Melk defined a Central European style, and in the later eighteenth century Christoph Willibald Gluck, Joseph Haydn and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1699" aria-label="Graz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johann Bernhard Fischer von Erlach"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4161 6004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4161 6004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1699">What was here in 1699? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
